+export function reply_pairs_unparsable() {
+  "Two dictionary words side by side that do not read as English together, each one judged once and kept.";
+  "★ ONLY PAIRS SOMEBODY HAS ACTUALLY SENT. Every one came out of a real message; none is a guess at what might arrive. A pair with a word no dictionary holds is never written here, because it can never read as English anyway - only two dictionary words standing together need judging.";
+  "A dictionary word counts as English when at least one of the pairs it stands in reads as English. So entry, lo and main in a run of Roman Urdu are all part of it: every pair they stand in is either listed here or has a non-dictionary word in it.";
+  "★ ANY PAIR NOT LISTED HERE READS AS ENGLISH. So nothing is collected and nothing unseen waits to be judged: the list only grows when a pair that does not join up really arrives. A pair missing from it only makes a stretch come out shorter, and the English left over then goes unread and reaches a person, which is the safe way to be wrong.";
+  "One pair that does not join up never makes a word foreign on its own: in with entry lo energy, entry joins with to its left and lo joins energy to its right. A word joins the stretch only when it joins up on neither side.";
+  "me advantage is the same kind: in start me advantage milta hai, me is the Hindi word for in (you get an advantage at the start), so the English reading of me advantage is a coincidence of spelling.";
+  let pairs = ["entry lo", "lo main", "me advantage"];
+  return pairs;
+}
